# From SŪTRADHĀRA — the durability register is complete. I was wrong to hold 0.5a, and I have unblocked it.

M0-T29 closed M0-T27's declared gap: **23 of 23 seed-overwritten columns modelled, zero cells
UNKNOWN.** A re-seed would move **260 cells across 96 assets**.

## The headline is reassuring, and it did not have to hunt for that

**Most campaign repairs are durable**, and the register says so plainly rather than manufacturing
risk to justify itself. `domain`, `rung`, `superseded_by`, `data_disposition`, `has_substeps`,
`has_writer`, `integrity_check_sql`, `clear_tables`, `service_health` and
`writer_timeout_seconds` are in **neither** of the seed's column lists. Two real exposures remain,
both already identified by M0-T27 and now independently re-measured.

## I was wrong about Phase 0.5a, and the correction runs in my favour — which is why I want it recorded

I held 0.5a on the grounds that `layer_index` and `layer_name` are in the `DO UPDATE` list, so a
re-seed would revert the repair the way it reverts `asset_kind`.

**The register refutes that. On all 41 divergent cells the SEED holds the CLEAN value and the
DATABASE holds the dirty one.** The risk runs the *opposite* way: a re-seed would **repair** these
columns, not revert them. A DB repair to the clean value is therefore durable, because the seed
agrees with it.

I have unblocked and dispatched it (M0-T26), with the premise flagged to the KĀRAKA as *my*
mistake and an instruction to verify it before writing rather than inherit a correction. I record
this because I asked you to rule on a hold that rested on an unmeasured assumption, and the
measurement went against me.

## The `?? 'data'` trap was load-bearing, and the register got it right

The `asset_kind` reversion count is **8, not 6** — because **not one of the eight seed entries
declares the key at all.** The default is an *active write*, not a no-op. Modelling an absent
column as "unchanged" would have reported **0** and produced a falsely reassuring register.

## What the register explicitly cannot settle — and this is the ownership question, sharpened

A re-seed moves **80 cells** of `count_sql` / `target_floor` / `depends_on`, and the register says
there is **no basis to decide which side is right**. That is your D-19 question with a number
attached rather than a principle.

Two consequences you should have before ruling:

1. **Measured floors live in a seed-owned column.** I7 requires `target_floor` be set to the
   measured achieved count, and §N.4 forbids fabricating one — but the seed owns that column, so
   every floor this campaign measures is written into something a re-seed overwrites. That is not
   a defect in any one repair; it is a structural collision between I7 and the seed's ownership,
   and it will recur at **every rung's stage 2**, not just in M0.
2. **The reverse repair is not available either.** Writing `scope='per_chart'` to the database
   does not stick. So for the seed-owned columns there is no DB-side fix at all — any durable
   correction is in the seed file or in a `CASE` guard. The choice is narrower than it looked.

## One finding I would act on soon, whichever way you rule

**A routine re-seed would deactivate `bg_sky_calendar`.** It is live `is_active=true`, but its
declared `target_table` is `bg_sky_events` — a relation that **does not exist in production** —
and the seed's pre-flight at `:3228` forces `is_active=false` for a missing table. The MR-06 guard
that protects against unwanted deactivation is RETIRED-keyed and does not cover this case.

This is the second consequence of that phantom `target_table`, which M0-T6 found earlier and which
is pinned to R0. The first was that every count and integrity check authored for the asset would
inherit a pointer to nothing. The second is that the asset switches itself off the next time
anyone seeds. Still R0's by layer and still untouched — but it is now a defect with a trigger
rather than a latent one.

Also worth knowing: **the MR-06 guard has never been exercised.** Its only live RETIRED row is
declared RETIRED in the seed too, so both branches of the guard agree and it has never had to
choose. A guard that has never had to choose is not yet a detector.

## One uncertainty the KĀRAKA raised about its own method, and I think it was right to

To model the seed without running it (D-13/D-17), it **sliced out the `ASSETS` literal, proved it
inert by machine, and evaluated that alone**, re-implementing `main()`'s derivations against cited
line numbers. It flags honestly that it is unsure whether evaluating an inertness-proven extracted
literal counts as "running the seed". My read is that it does not — nothing executed the module,
no `main()` ran, no connection existed — but it is your prohibition and it asked rather than
assumed, which is the behaviour I want.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
